  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nSky cover was clearing out nicely after this morning's round of \nshowers and weak thunderstorms. 18Z surface observations were \nshowing temperatures warming quickly through the mid to upper 80s. \nWinds were light and variable across much of southwest and west \ncentral Kansas, and as such, low level convergence was negligible. \nThere is a very low probability of renewed thunderstorm development \nthis afternoon/early this evening, but if any isolated storm(s) do \ndevelop, they would form pretty far west out near the Colorado line. \nThe much greater probability for more organized thunderstorm \nactivity will be out across southeastern Colorado this evening. HREF \nensemble system showed the best signal for our DDC CWA across the \nsouthwest from Elkhart to Coolidge/Syracuse, roughly, and this is \nwhere POPs will be highest tonight (30 to 50%, highest along the \nColorado line). There is a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) of severe \nstorms from the latest SPC Day One outlook for a low-end severe wind \nand large hail risk with the strongest storm. Given the fairly weak \nlow level winds, a well-organized mesoscale convective system (MCS) \nis unlikely through the night, which is why POPs drop off quite a \nbit farther east overnight.\n\nOn Thursday, the northwest flow pattern aloft will continue as the \nsummer subtropical mid level high will be well off to our west-\nsouthwest over the Four Corners region. A weak surface high will \npush east into northeastern Kansas and into Missouri with surface \nwinds across western Kansas becoming southeasterly again. The best \nconvergence Thursday/Thursday Evening will shift back to the \nnorthwest across northeastern Colorado, far northwestern Kansas, and \nsouthwestern Nebraska which is the most likely region for surface-\nbased strong/locally severe storms. Should a small MCS evolve \nThursday evening, it would move southeast toward the I-70 corridor, \nwhich is where POPs will be focused for Thursday Night period, \nalbeit only 20-30%. \n\nAs far as temperatures go, the southerly winds will result in hotter \ntemperatures redeveloping with lower-mid 90s likely for highs \nThursday and even hotter 98-102F for highs most areas Friday \nafternoon. An upper level jet streak will round the upper level \nridge Friday into Saturday, which will focus a better chance for \norganized MCS activity mainly northeast of our area across Nebraska \ninto the northeastern half of Kansas. This jet streak diving \nsoutheast across the Northern Plains into the Upper Midwest will \nalso push another front south into Kansas. This jet streak and front \nare now a bit stronger in the models, pushing northeasterly low \nlevel winds deeper into Kansas Friday Night into Saturday. Despite \nthe hot temperatures in the latest forecast, the NBM has not \nentirely caught up to this latest change in the models for the \nweekend, so expect a drop in forecast temperature going into the \nweekend, especially the northeastern third to half of the DDC CWA \nSaturday and perhaps Sunday. We may not realize the widespread 100+ \nafternoon highs over the weekend as currently forecast, so keep \nchecking back as confidence increases in the weekend front \nstrength/timing.",
